The U.S. financial clearing architecture operates as a closed, deterministic causal pipeline. Legislative authority forms the bounding boundary condition $\mathcal{C}_{\text{law}}$, regulatory agencies define the admissible parameter manifold $\Omega_{\text{reg}}$, SIFMU governance executes model and operational control $\mathbf{K}_{\text{gov}}$, and central counterparties compute continuous, netted, and collateralized risk transfers $\mathbf{T}_{\text{settle}}$.

